<h4>Jain Councils and Literature</h4>

<p>The <strong>First Jain Council</strong> was held at <strong>Pataliputra</strong> (c. 300 BCE) under Chandragupta Maurya's patronage, led by Sthulabhadra, to compile the sacred texts (the 12 Angas). It is said that only 11 Angas survived because the 12th (Drishtivada) was lost. The <strong>Second Council</strong> was at <strong>Valabhi</strong> (Gujarat, c. 456 CE) under Devardhigani Kshamashramana, where the Jain canon was finally written down on palm leaves. <strong>Jain Canonical Texts</strong>: The 12 Angas (Acharanga-sutra - monastic rules, Sutrakritanga - heretical doctrines, Sthananga - numerical lists, Samavayanga - syncretic lists, Vyakhyaprajnapati/Bhagavati-sutra - the largest, containing the 16 Mahajanapadas list, etc.), 12 Upangas, 10 Prakirnakas (miscellaneous texts), 6 Chedasutras (disciplinary texts), 4 Mulasutras (basic texts), and the Nandisutra/Avashyaka (essential rituals). Non-canonical but important texts: <strong>Tattvartha Sutra</strong> (by Umasvati/Umasvami, c. 2nd-5th cent. CE - the definitive systematic Jain text, accepted by both sects), <strong>Shatkhandagama</strong> (Digambara doctrinal text by Pushpadanta and Bhutabali), <strong>Yogashastra</strong> and <strong>Yogadrishtisamuccaya</strong> (by Haribhadra Suri), <strong>Kalpa Sutra</strong> (by Bhadrabahu - contains biographies of the Tirthankaras and the life of Mahavira). <strong>Prakrit</strong> was the language of the Jain scriptures, not Sanskrit — the Ardha-Magadhi Prakrit for the Svetambara canon.</p>

<h4>Contributions of Buddhism and Jainism</h4>

<ul>
<li><strong>Social reform</strong>: Both rejected caste hierarchy and preached social equality. The Buddha said 'Not by birth does one become a Brahmin, but by deeds.' Jainism taught that all souls are inherently equal - the potential for liberation exists in all beings.</li>
<li><strong>Language and literature</strong>: Both used <strong>Prakrit</strong> (and later Pali) to reach common people, breaking the Brahmanical monopoly on Sanskrit. Pali became the sacred language of Theravada Buddhism; Ardha-Magadhi for Jain canon. Mahayana Buddhism later adopted Sanskrit.</li>
<li><strong>Art and architecture</strong>: Inspired magnificent monuments - stupas (Sanchi, Bharhut, Amaravati), cave temples (Ajanta, Ellora, Karla), pillar edicts (Ashoka), universities (Nalanda, Vikramashila, Taxila). Jain temple cities at Dilwara (Mount Abu, white marble), Palitana (Shatrunjaya hills, 863 temples), Shravanabelagola (57-ft Bahubali/Gommateshwara monolith, 981 CE).</li>
<li><strong>Institutions of learning</strong>: Buddhist universities (Nalanda - 10,000 monks, 2000 teachers, 3 libraries - c. 5th-12th cent. CE; Vikramashila - 108 teachers; Odantapuri; Somapura in Bengal) attracted students from China, Tibet, Korea, Sri Lanka, Central Asia. Xuanzang (Hiuen Tsang) studied at Nalanda for 5 years (c. 630 CE), recording its magnificence: 'The whole establishment is surrounded by a brick wall... one principal entrance opens into the great gate... the richly adorned towers and the fairy-like turrets seem to touch the clouds.'</li>
<li><strong>Spread of Indian culture abroad</strong>: Buddhism carried Indian culture, art, and philosophy to Central Asia (Gandhara, Bamiyan - giant Buddha statues, 55m and 38m; the Silk Route), China (Kumarajiva, Bodhidharma, Faxian, Xuanzang), Tibet (Padmasambhava, Atisha), Korea (Monk Hyecho), Japan (Prince Shotoku's 17-Article Constitution - based on Buddhism), Sri Lanka (Mahinda and Sanghamitta - Ashoka's children), and Southeast Asia (Angkor Wat, Borobudur, Shwedagon Pagoda).</li>
</ul>

<div class="warn-box"><div class="warn-title">Common UPSC Mistake: Buddhism vs Jainism Doctrines</div><div class="warn-text"><strong>Don't confuse:</strong> Buddhism rejects the existence of the soul (anatman) as the defining feature that separates it from all other Indian religions. Jainism, by contrast, affirms the existence of infinite souls (jivas) and makes the soul the central focus - the goal is to free the soul from karmic matter. The Buddha rejected extreme asceticism as the Middle Way; Mahavira embraced it fully. The Buddha accepted the existence of gods (devas) but said they were also trapped in samsara; Jainism is consistently non-theistic (gods exist but are subordinate to the Tirthankaras and are also subject to karma).</div></div>

<div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">Essay Application</div><div class="ex-text">For UPSC essays on 'Tradition vs Modernity', 'Science and Spirituality', or 'Non-violence in World Affairs', consider: Buddhism introduced an empirical, experiential approach to spirituality (test the doctrine like a goldsmith tests gold) that resonates with modern scientific thinking. Jainism's anekantavada is a profound philosophical foundation for tolerance and pluralism that is highly relevant in today's world of ideological conflict. The concept of ahimsa directly influenced Mahatma Gandhi's satyagraha, which in turn inspired Martin Luther King Jr. and Nelson Mandela.</div></div>

